WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.778
Batch size tuning for LLM inference is about matching the model, runtime, hardware,

00:00:04.778 --> 00:00:06.217
context length, and workload.

00:00:06.217 --> 00:00:09.456
It is not a contest to maximize every setting.

00:00:09.456 --> 00:00:14.494
A larger value can improve throughput in one situation and damage latency, memory stability,

00:00:14.494 --> 00:00:15.933
or reliability in another.

00:00:15.933 --> 00:00:21.331
The right setting is the one that supports the way the system is actually used.

00:00:21.331 --> 00:00:24.570
The most important distinction is between prefill and decode.

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:27.089
Prefill processes the prompt and existing context.

00:00:27.089 --> 00:00:28.528
Decode produces new tokens.

00:00:28.528 --> 00:00:30.327
Batch settings, prompt length, concurrency,

00:00:30.327 --> 00:00:32.846
and memory pressure affect those phases differently.

00:00:32.846 --> 00:00:36.805
That is why first-token latency, tokens per second, total response time,

00:00:36.805 --> 00:00:40.763
and requests per second all tell different parts of the story.

00:00:40.763 --> 00:00:43.282
Good tuning is measured, conservative, and repeatable.

00:00:43.282 --> 00:00:45.081
Start with a realistic workload.

00:00:45.081 --> 00:00:47.240
Change one setting at a time.

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:50.119
Track memory, utilization, temperature, errors, latency, and throughput.

00:00:50.119 --> 00:00:52.278
Keep enough headroom for normal variation,

00:00:52.278 --> 00:00:56.956
because a configuration that barely survives a test can fail under real use.

00:00:56.956 --> 00:00:59.835
For local interactive systems, prioritize responsiveness and stability.

00:00:59.835 --> 00:01:04.153
For API serving, balance user latency with total throughput, fairness, queue behavior,

00:01:04.153 --> 00:01:04.873
and backpressure.

00:01:04.873 --> 00:01:09.911
In both cases, document the settings that work for each model and hardware profile.

00:01:09.911 --> 00:01:13.149
The goal is reliable inference that feels predictable, observable,

00:01:13.149 --> 00:01:14.949
and appropriate for the workload.

00:01:14.949 --> 00:01:18.547
The durable lesson is that inference tuning is operational engineering,

00:01:18.547 --> 00:01:20.346
not a magic number hunt.

00:01:20.346 --> 00:01:24.665
The same model can need different settings on a laptop, a workstation,

00:01:24.665 --> 00:01:26.104
and a shared service.

00:01:26.104 --> 00:01:30.782
What carries across environments is the method: define the workload, measure the phases,

00:01:30.782 --> 00:01:35.460
control memory pressure, and keep the system observable enough to explain its behavior.
